Subject: [PATCH 23/24] Two more packets: the enforcement lane and the security
 lane, each one page, each built by script
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The criminal lane has had a single-page packet since this morning; four lanes were
promised the same builder. These are the second and third, and they are the two the
sweep hit hardest.

packets/build_enforcement_packet.py assembles packets/enforcement.md. It inlines, in
reading order: the sweep's F2, F3 and F4 with the lane's remaining register; OPEN
QUESTION 4 and its Colorado tier-placement donor; CUREs 9, 10, 14, 15 and 16, each
with the donor material the intake of 23–24 August added to it (the select-agent
comparator to CURE 10's four controls, S. 1792's respond-and-cure echo to CURE 15,
the named witness and the distillation-farm second class to CURE 16); and, from the
state enforcement record, section 5 alone — what the live actions do to OPEN QUESTION
4, in both directions. The actions themselves are not restated: MAP's rule that no
other file may restate an enforcement action holds, and the packet links out.

packets/build_security_packet.py assembles packets/security.md, and that lane needed a
different shape. It is the one with nothing drafted in answer, so there is no queue of
repairs to verify; the packet says so in the ask, inlines F5 and F6 with the lane's
register, reproduces Part 6 of the regulations whole — the six control objectives are
what the findings are about, and the reviewer needs them beside the incident's five
contributing factors — and then carries CURE 10 and its 42 C.F.R. § 73.11 comparator
as the federal answer sheet to mark six new objectives against. The statute itself is
still never reproduced.

Wiring: the packets index lists all three and the count of unbuilt lanes drops to two;
the REVIEWERS.md lane preamble links all three; the queue's header line, which pointed
at the criminal packet alone, points at the index instead.

Anchors: links to record.md chunks 3 and 5, to errata E2, E3, E6, E28 and E29, and to
the companion's READ FIRST index use full heading slugs. The short forms in use
elsewhere (#chunk-3, #chunk-5, #e28, #e29 in REVIEWERS.md; the companion's
#read-first--open-items-… in REVIEWERS.md and the criminal builder) do not resolve on
GitHub; that is left for a separate decision rather than fixed in passing.
